1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:03,073
One country, one tiny island, one
citizens' campaign,

2
00:00:03,113 --> 00:00:05,763
one energy giant and one Indian company:

3
00:00:05,803 --> 00:00:10,245
these are the five winners of the
Earthshot Prize 2026.

4
00:00:10,413 --> 00:00:14,215
What did they do? For each one: who, what
they did,

5
00:00:14,255 --> 00:00:15,752
and how it helps.

6
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:16,600
Let's decode.

7
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,947
First, Bhutan won Protect and Restore
Nature,

8
00:00:19,987 --> 00:00:25,207
and it is the first country ever to win
this prize.

9
00:00:25,247 --> 00:00:31,423
Its Restore Bhutan plan will revive 50,000
hectares of damaged Himalayan land,

10
00:00:31,463 --> 00:00:38,117
saving nearly 2 million tonnes of carbon
dioxide and protecting water for about one

11
00:00:38,157 --> 00:00:42,500
point six billion people downstream,
including us in India.

12
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:46,854
Second, our own Mahindra Last Mile
Mobility won Clean Our Air.

13
00:00:46,894 --> 00:00:52,000
It made electric autos cheap to buy and to
run for ordinary drivers,

14
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:57,145
and it has put more than 4 lakh electric
vehicles on Indian roads,

15
00:00:57,185 --> 00:00:59,600
cutting smoke in our crowded cities.

16
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,344
Third, Niue won Revive Our Oceans.

17
00:01:02,384 --> 00:01:08,746
This Pacific island has only about 1,700
people, yet its Ocean Wide Trust,

18
00:01:08,786 --> 00:01:12,404
with a new way of investing in
conservation,

19
00:01:12,444 --> 00:01:16,600
protects an ocean area larger than the
United Kingdom.

20
00:01:16,640 --> 00:01:20,472
Fourth, PFAS-Free France won Build a
Waste-Free World.

21
00:01:20,512 --> 00:01:24,344
PFAS are the forever chemicals found in
non-stick pans,

22
00:01:24,384 --> 00:01:26,280
waterproof clothes and food packaging,

23
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:30,540
and they barely break down in nature or in
our bodies.

24
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:34,025
Backed by more than 1 lakh 40,000
citizens,

25
00:01:34,065 --> 00:01:40,300
this campaign helped France pass a 2025
law to phase out many of them.

26
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,084
Fifth, Denmark's Ørsted won Fix Our
Climate.

27
00:01:43,124 --> 00:01:46,663
It was once an oil, gas and coal company;

28
00:01:46,703 --> 00:01:51,038
it quit oil and gas and became an
offshore-wind giant,

29
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:54,300
proving that even an energy company can
change.

30
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:57,070
Prelims trap, from those forever
chemicals:

31
00:01:57,110 --> 00:02:02,609
some of them are already restricted under
the Stockholm Convention on persistent

32
00:02:02,649 --> 00:02:05,379
organic pollutants, which India has
joined.

33
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:09,072
Do not confuse it with the Rotterdam
Convention,

34
00:02:09,112 --> 00:02:13,689
on consent before trading hazardous
chemicals, or the Basel Convention,

35
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:15,997
on hazardous waste crossing borders.

36
00:02:16,037 --> 00:02:17,000
Remember this!

37
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:23,210
Mains question: can India's coal-heavy
power companies make a shift like Ørsted?

38
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:29,582
Write your opinion in the comments, and
follow for one exam trap every day!

